  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 2.07 — Spherical and Elliptic Geometry\n\nThe last of the three constant-curvature geometries, and the one everybody has already met without being told it was geometry: navigation, astronomy, and the fact that the shortest flight from London to Los Angeles goes near Greenland.\n\n$$S^2 = \\{ v \\in \\mathbb R^3 : |v| = 1 \\}, \\qquad G = O(3).$$\n\nWe built this space in 2.01 as the homogeneous space $SO(3)/SO(2)$; now we put a metric on it and do geometry.\n\nAlmost everything is the mirror image of 2.06, with the sign of the curvature flipped:\n\n| | Hyperbolic (2.06) | Spherical (here) |\n|---|---|---|\n| curvature | $K = -1$ | $K = +1$ |\n| parallels through a point | infinitely many | **none** |\n| angle sum of a triangle | $< \\pi$ | $> \\pi$ |\n| area | $\\pi - (\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)$ | $(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma) - \\pi$ |\n| largest triangle | area $\\pi$ | area $2\\pi$ |\n| total area | infinite | $4\\pi$ |\n\nThe area law is **Girard's theorem** (1629), and it predates the hyperbolic version by two centuries — the positively curved case was easier to find because the model was sitting in the sky.\n\nOne genuine difference, not just a sign: the sphere fails Euclid's *first* postulate as well as the fifth. Two antipodal points are joined by infinitely many geodesics, not a unique one. Repairing that by identifying antipodes gives **elliptic geometry** — and the resulting space is $\\mathbb{RP}^2$, the projective plane of 2.02, now carrying a metric.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. Geodesics are great circles\n\nThe metric on $S^2$ is the one inherited from $\\mathbb R^3$: the length of a path on the sphere is its length in space.\n\n**Distance.** For unit vectors $u, v$, the geodesic distance is the angle between them:\n\n$$d(u,v) = \\arccos(u \\cdot v) \\in [0, \\pi].$$\n\nThe dot product from 1.01 does the work again, now measuring distance on a curved space.\n\n**Geodesics are great circles** — intersections of the sphere with planes through the centre. The argument is pure symmetry, and it is the cleanest one available:\n\nGiven $u, v$, let $\\Pi$ be a plane through $0$, $u$ and $v$, and let $\\sigma$ be the reflection of $\\mathbb R^3$ in $\\Pi$. Then $\\sigma$ is an isometry of the sphere fixing $u$ and $v$, and its fixed-point set on the sphere is exactly the great circle $\\Pi \\cap S^2$. A shortest path from $u$ to $v$ must be unique (locally), so $\\sigma$ must map it to itself, so it must lie in the fixed set. Hence it lies on the great circle. $\\blacksquare$\n\nThat argument is worth noticing because it is *reused verbatim* in Riemannian geometry: geodesics of a symmetric space lie in the fixed-point sets of the symmetries. It is also the same \"reflect and see what is fixed\" reasoning that classified plane isometries in 1.02.\n\n**No parallels.** Any two distinct great circles intersect — in a pair of antipodal points — because any two distinct planes through the origin meet in a line, and that line meets the sphere twice. So through a point off a given geodesic there is *no* geodesic missing it. The fifth postulate fails in the opposite direction from 2.06.\n\n**Maximum distance.** $d(u, -u) = \\pi$: antipodal points are as far apart as it is possible to be. The space is compact, of total area $4\\pi$, with no boundary — unlike $\\mathbb H$, which was unbounded and infinite in extent.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\nvnorm(a)    := sqrt(a . a)$\nunitv(a)    := ratsimp( a / vnorm(a) )$\nsdist(u,v)  := acos( ratsimp(u . v) )$\n\n/* Points on the sphere, given by longitude λ and colatitude θ */\nspt(θ, λ) := [sin(θ)*cos(λ), sin(θ)*sin(λ), cos(θ)]$\n\nN : [0,0,1]$   E1 : [1,0,0]$   E2 : [0,1,0]$\nprint(\"d(north pole, equator point) = \", sdist(N, E1), \" = π/2\")$\nprint(\"d(E1, E2)                    = \", sdist(E1, E2), \" = π/2\")$\nprint(\"d(N, −N)                     = \", sdist(N, -N), \" = π  (antipodal, maximal)\")$\n\n/* A great circle through two points: the plane through 0, u, v has normal u×v */\ngc_normal(u, v) := unitv( cross3(u, v) )$\non_gc(n, p) := is( ratsimp(n . p) = 0 )$\n\nnrm : gc_normal(E1, E2)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"great circle through E1 and E2 has normal \", nrm, \"  (the north pole)\")$\nprint(\"   E1 on it? \", on_gc(nrm, E1), \"   E2 on it? \", on_gc(nrm, E2),\n      \"   N on it? \", on_gc(nrm, N))$\nprint(\"   — so that great circle is the equator\")$\n\n/* Two distinct great circles always meet, in an antipodal pair */\nn1 : unitv([0,0,1])$        /* the equator            */\nn2 : unitv([1,1,1])$        /* some tilted great circle */\nmeet_pts : unitv( cross3(n1, n2) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"two great circles with normals \", n1, \" and \", float(n2), \":\")$\nprint(\"   meet at \", float(meet_pts), \"  and its antipode\")$\nprint(\"   on both? \", on_gc(n1, meet_pts), \" and \", on_gc(n2, meet_pts))$\nprint(\"   ⟹ there are NO parallels: every pair of geodesics intersects\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "d(north pole, equator point) =  %pi/2  = π/2\nd(E1, E2)                    =  %pi/2  = π/2\nd(N, −N)                     =  %pi  = π  (antipodal, maximal)\ngreat circle through E1 and E2 has normal  [0,0,1]   (the north pole)\n   E1 on it?  true    E2 on it?  true    N on it?  false\n   — so that great circle is the equator\ntwo great circles with normals  [0,0,1]  and\n                               [0.5773502691896258,0.5773502691896258,\n                                0.5773502691896258] :\n   meet at  [-0.7071067811865475,0.7071067811865475,0.0]   and its antipode\n   on both?  true  and  true\n   ⟹ there are NO parallels: every pair of geodesics intersects"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. Girard's theorem: area is angle excess\n\n> **Girard (1629).** A spherical triangle with angles $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ has area\n> $$\\text{Area} = \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma - \\pi.$$\n\nThe proof is a counting argument with **lunes**. A lune is the region between two half great circles meeting at angle $\\alpha$; since a full turn $2\\pi$ sweeps the whole sphere of area $4\\pi$, a lune of angle $\\alpha$ has area $2\\alpha$.\n\nThe three great circles carrying the sides of $T$ cut the sphere into eight triangles. Take the three lunes $L_\\alpha, L_\\beta, L_\\gamma$ containing $T$. Together they cover a full hemisphere, but they cover $T$ itself three times instead of once — two extra copies. Hence\n\n$$2\\alpha + 2\\beta + 2\\gamma = 2\\pi + 2\\,\\text{Area}(T),$$\n\nwhich rearranges to the theorem. $\\blacksquare$\n\n**To check this independently** we need an area formula that does not already assume Girard. The **Van Oosterom–Strackee** formula gives the solid angle subtended by three unit vectors directly:\n\n$$\\tan\\frac{\\Omega}{2} = \\frac{\\lvert a \\cdot (b \\times c)\\rvert}{1 + a\\cdot b + b\\cdot c + c\\cdot a},$$\n\nand on the unit sphere solid angle *is* area. Note the numerator: it is the scalar triple product from 1.01, the signed volume of the parallelepiped — measuring the area of a spherical triangle.\n\nThree consequences mirror 2.06 exactly:\n\n- **The angle sum always exceeds $\\pi$**, and the excess is the area.\n- **Triangles are bounded**: area $< 2\\pi$, approached as the triangle swells to a hemisphere.\n- **Similar triangles are congruent** — angles determine area, so there is again an absolute unit of length. The sphere's radius sets the scale, and on a general surface that role is played by the curvature.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Interior angle at vertex A of the spherical triangle ABC:\n   the angle between the planes OAB and OAC, i.e. between their normals. */\nvertex_angle(A, B, C) := block([n1, n2],\n  n1 : cross3(A, B),\n  n2 : cross3(A, C),\n  acos( ratsimp( (n1 . n2)/(vnorm(n1)*vnorm(n2)) ) ) )$\n\n/* Van Oosterom–Strackee: area of the spherical triangle, computed WITHOUT\n   reference to the angles — an independent check on Girard. */\ntri_area_vos(A, B, C) := block([num, den],\n  num : abs( A . cross3(B, C) ),\n  den : 1 + A . B + B . C + C . A,\n  2 * atan2( num, den ) )$\n\ncheck(A, B, C, label) := block([al, be, ga, exc, ar],\n  al : vertex_angle(A,B,C), be : vertex_angle(B,C,A), ga : vertex_angle(C,A,B),\n  exc : al + be + ga - %pi,\n  ar  : tri_area_vos(A,B,C),\n  print(\"\"),\n  print(label),\n  print(\"   angles        = \", float(al), \", \", float(be), \", \", float(ga)),\n  print(\"   angle sum     = \", float(al+be+ga), \"   (π = \", float(%pi), \")\"),\n  print(\"   excess        = \", float(exc)),\n  print(\"   area (V-O-S)  = \", float(ar)),\n  print(\"   difference    = \", float(ar - exc)) )$\n\n/* The octant: three right angles, area should be 1/8 of 4π = π/2 */\ncheck(E1, E2, N, \"octant triangle (1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1)\")$\n\n/* A smaller, scalene triangle */\nA2 : unitv([1, 0, 3])$  B2 : unitv([0, 1, 3])$  C2 : unitv([1, 1, 5])$\ncheck(A2, B2, C2, \"a small scalene triangle — nearly Euclidean\")$\n\n/* A large triangle, approaching a hemisphere */\nA3 : unitv([1, 0, 1/10])$  B3 : unitv([-1/2, 1, 1/10])$  C3 : unitv([-1/2, -1, 1/10])$\ncheck(A3, B3, C3, \"a large triangle — angle sum well above π\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"area = angle excess in every case: Girard's theorem ∎\")$\nprint(\"total sphere area = 4π = \", float(4*%pi),\n      \";  a hemisphere = 2π = \", float(2*%pi), \" bounds triangle area\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "octant triangle (1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1)\n   angles        =  1.5707963267948966 ,  1.5707963267948966 ,\n                   1.5707963267948966\n   angle sum     =  4.71238898038469    (π =  3.141592653589793 )\n   excess        =  1.5707963267948966\n   area (V-O-S)  =  1.5707963267948966\n   difference    =  0.0\na small scalene triangle — nearly Euclidean\n   angles        =  0.19512756790603397 ,  0.19512756790603397 ,\n                   2.76134144689686\n   angle sum     =  3.151596582708928    (π =  3.141592653589793 )\n   excess        =  0.010003929119134503\n   area (V-O-S)  =  0.010003929119135625\n   difference    =  1.1223660889569942e-15\na large triangle — angle sum well above π\n   angles        =  2.8452689982557184 ,  2.810611415421446 ,\n                   2.810611415421446\n   angle sum     =  8.466491829098612    (π =  3.141592653589793 )\n   excess        =  5.3248991755088175\n   area (V-O-S)  =  5.324899175508817\n   difference    =  -8.881784197001252e-16\narea = angle excess in every case: Girard's theorem ∎\ntotal sphere area = 4π =  12.566370614359172 ;  a hemisphere = 2π =\n                         6.283185307179586  bounds triangle area"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Elliptic geometry: the sphere is not quite right\n\nThe sphere fails Euclid's **first** postulate, not just the fifth. Through two antipodal points there pass infinitely many geodesics — every great circle through the north pole also passes through the south pole. \"Two points determine a line\" is false.\n\nThe repair is to declare antipodal points to be the same point:\n\n$$\\text{elliptic plane} \\;=\\; S^2/(v \\sim -v) \\;=\\; \\mathbb{RP}^2 .$$\n\nThis is exactly the projective plane of 2.02, now equipped with a metric. The consequences:\n\n- **Any two points lie on a unique geodesic** — the first postulate is restored, because the two intersection points of two great circles have become one point.\n- **Any two geodesics meet in exactly one point** — which is the projective incidence statement of 2.02, now with a metric attached.\n- Total area is $2\\pi$, half the sphere's, and the maximum distance is $\\pi/2$.\n- It is **non-orientable**, as noted in 2.02.\n\nSo the third classical geometry is the projective plane with a metric — and the fact that its incidence structure was already what 2.02 derived is not a coincidence. This is the Cayley–Klein construction once more: fix an *imaginary* conic in $\\mathbb{RP}^2$, keep the projectivities preserving it, and you get $O(3)$ acting by isometries. Fixing a real conic gave hyperbolic geometry; fixing an imaginary one gives elliptic.\n\n| Fixed conic in $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ | Subgroup | Geometry | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| real, non-degenerate | $PSL(2,\\mathbb R)$ | hyperbolic | $-1$ |\n| degenerate (double line) | affine → Euclidean | Euclidean | $0$ |\n| imaginary | $O(3)$ | elliptic | $+1$ |\n\nThree geometries, one construction, distinguished only by the reality of a conic.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Antipodal points are joined by infinitely many geodesics --- */\n/* Every great circle through N also passes through −N. */\nprint(\"great circles through the north pole, for several normals:\")$\nfor λ in [0, %pi/6, %pi/3, %pi/2] do block([nn],\n  nn : [cos(λ), sin(λ), 0],          /* normal ⟂ to the z-axis ⟹ circle through N */\n  print(\"   normal \", float(nn), \"   passes through N? \", on_gc(nn, N),\n        \"   through −N? \", on_gc(nn, -N)) )$\nprint(\"   ⟹ infinitely many geodesics join N to −N: the first postulate fails\")$\nprint(\"   identifying v ~ −v repairs it, and gives RP² — elliptic geometry\")$\n\n/* --- How the angle sum grows with size --- */\n/* An equilateral triangle with vertices at colatitude θ, 120° apart. */\neqtri(θ) := [ spt(θ, 0), spt(θ, 2*%pi/3), spt(θ, 4*%pi/3) ]$\n\nangsum(θ) := block([T, A, B, C],\n  T : eqtri(θ), [A,B,C] : T,\n  float( vertex_angle(A,B,C) + vertex_angle(B,C,A) + vertex_angle(C,A,B) ) )$\nareaof(θ) := block([T, A, B, C],\n  T : eqtri(θ), [A,B,C] : T,\n  float( tri_area_vos(A,B,C) ) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   θ      angle sum     area      sum − π     (should be equal)\")$\nfor k : 1 thru 7 do block([θ0],\n  θ0 : float(k*%pi/16),\n  print(\"  \", θ0, \"   \", angsum(θ0), \"   \", areaof(θ0), \"   \",\n        angsum(θ0) - float(%pi)) )$\n\nthetas : makelist(float(k*%pi/200), k, 1, 99)$\nplot2d([ [discrete, thetas, map(angsum, thetas)],\n         [discrete, thetas, map(lambda([t], float(%pi) + areaof(t)), thetas)],\n         [discrete, [0.0, float(%pi/2)], [float(%pi), float(%pi)]] ],\n       [x, 0, 1.6], [y, 2.9, 7],\n       [legend, \"angle sum\", \"π + area\", \"π (Euclidean value)\"],\n       [xlabel, \"colatitude θ of the vertices\"], [ylabel, \"\"],\n       [title, \"spherical triangles: angle sum exceeds π by exactly the area\"])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "great circles through the north pole, for several normals:\n   normal  [1.0,0.0,0.0]    passes through N?  true    through −N?  true\n   normal  [0.8660254037844386,0.5,0.0]    passes through N?  true\n             through −N?  true\n   normal  [0.5,0.8660254037844386,0.0]    passes through N?  true\n             through −N?  true\n   normal  [0.0,1.0,0.0]    passes through N?  true    through −N?  true\n   ⟹ infinitely many geodesics join N to −N: the first postulate fails\n   identifying v ~ −v repairs it, and gives RP² — elliptic geometry\n   θ      angle sum     area      sum − π     (should be equal)\n   0.19634954084936207     3.192242693382311     0.05065003979251934\n  0.05065003979251781\n   0.39269908169872414     3.3512482438738935     0.2096555902840991\n  0.2096555902841004\n   0.5890486225480862     3.641637846753246     0.500045193163452\n  0.5000451931634529\n   0.7853981633974483     4.108315218013697     0.9667225644239038\n  0.9667225644239039\n   0.9817477042468103     4.827722863951484     1.6861302103616913\n  1.686130210361691\n   1.1780972450961724     5.9127393840273905     2.771146730437598\n  2.7711467304375974\n   1.3744467859455345     7.469614316148494     4.3280216625587045\n  4.328021662558701"
          ]
