    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| | Value |\n|---|---|\n| space | $S^2$, or $\\mathbb{RP}^2 = S^2/\\pm$ for elliptic |\n| group | $O(3)$ |\n| distance | $d(u,v) = \\arccos(u\\cdot v)$ |\n| geodesics | great circles |\n| parallels | none — every two geodesics meet |\n| area | $(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma) - \\pi$ (Girard) |\n| curvature | $K = +1$ |\n\nGirard's theorem was checked against the Van Oosterom–Strackee solid-angle formula — an area computation that never mentions the angles — and agreed to machine precision on a right-angled octant, a nearly-Euclidean small triangle, and a large one. The octant came out at exactly $\\pi/2$, one eighth of $4\\pi$.\n\n## Stage 2 complete: the trichotomy\n\nThe seven notebooks of this stage were one long argument. Starting from Euclidean geometry, we removed structure until almost nothing was left, then rebuilt three different geometries by putting back one piece in three different ways.\n\n| | Hyperbolic | Euclidean | Spherical / elliptic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| curvature $K$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $+1$ |\n| parallels through a point | infinitely many | exactly one | none |\n| angle sum | $< \\pi$ | $= \\pi$ | $> \\pi$ |\n| **area vs angles** | $\\pi - \\sum\\theta_i$ | — | $\\sum\\theta_i - \\pi$ |\n| model | $\\mathbb H$, $\\mathbb D$ | $\\mathbb R^2$ | $S^2$, $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ |\n| group | $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ | $E(2)$ | $O(3)$ |\n| fixed conic | real | degenerate | imaginary |\n| total area | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $4\\pi$ (or $2\\pi$) |\n\nEvery row is a single statement read three ways, and the unifying one is\n\n$$\\sum \\theta_i - \\pi \\;=\\; \\int\\!\\!\\int_T K \\, dA .$$\n\nFor constant $K \\in \\{-1, 0, +1\\}$ this reproduces all three columns. That identity has now appeared three times in this journey in different disguises — as the Diophantine trichotomy $\\sum 1/n_i \\gtrless 1$ classifying symmetry groups in 1.04, as the hyperbolic area law in 2.06, and as Girard's theorem here.\n\n## Where Klein stops and Riemann starts\n\nThese three are the *only* homogeneous surfaces — the only ones where a transformation group is large enough to define the geometry, which is what the Erlangen programme requires. Every other surface is lumpy, has no useful symmetry group, and lies outside Klein's framework entirely.\n\nStage 3 abandons homogeneity. Curvature stops being a constant chosen from $\\{-1,0,+1\\}$ and becomes a **function on the surface**, computed locally at each point from the metric. The three geometries above survive as the special cases where that function happens to be constant, and the identity above survives as the **Gauss–Bonnet theorem** (3.08), now with a genuine integral rather than a multiplication.\n\n## Next\n\n**Stage 3 opens with 3.01 — Curves, Arc Length and Curvature**, starting over with the simplest curved objects: curves in $\\mathbb R^3$, where curvature is a single number measuring the rate of turning.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Chapter 2 is spherical geometry, with Corollary 2.12 giving the area of a spherical $n$-gon as $\\theta_1 + \\cdots + \\theta_n - (n-2)\\pi$ — Girard generalised — and Theorem 2.13 on Euler's polyhedron formula.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Chapter 8 for polyhedra and their symmetry groups on the sphere.\n- Berger, M. (1987) *Geometry II*. Berlin: Springer. Chapter 18 for spherical and elliptic geometry, and the Cayley–Klein classification by a fixed conic.\n- Van Brummelen, G. (2013) *Heavenly Mathematics: The Forgotten Art of Spherical Trigonometry*. Princeton: Princeton University Press. The historical and computational side — navigation and astronomy, where this material originated.\n\nGirard's theorem is due to Albert Girard (1629); the result is sometimes attributed to Thomas Harriot, who found it earlier but did not publish.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}